</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each spike corresponds to a distinct escalation in the Hormuz conflict: the June 17 ceasefire signing, Iran''s June 23 re-closure of the strait, and Tuesday''s blockade reinstatement. The index has not closed above 20 in this window despite the war reaching its most severe point yet.</p>
<h2>The Precedent That Isn''t Quite a Precedent</h2>
<p>The comparison every advisor''s client will reach for is 2022, when markets initially shrugged off the early days of the Russia-Ukraine invasion before repricing sharply within a week. The difference here is repetition. Ukraine was a single shock the market had to learn to price in real time. The Hormuz conflict has given markets three full cycles of escalation and resolution to learn from, and each cycle has taught the same lesson: this fades. That lesson may be correct until the day it isn''t, and there is no reliable way to know in advance which cycle breaks the pattern.</p>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Most clients are not anxious right now, and that itself is worth naming. They have watched oil headlines escalate for months without their portfolio statements moving much, and the dominant feeling is a kind of tuned-out reassurance rather than genuine confidence. A smaller group, usually the more data literate clients, have noticed the VIX staying low and are quietly wondering whether that is a green light or a warning sign they are missing.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The volatility index has actually stayed calm through this week''s escalation, and that''s worth understanding rather than just taking as good news. Markets have been through three rounds of this since February, and each time the initial reaction has faded faster. Oil prices are still moving because that market has a direct link to the shipping risk. Broader equity markets have largely stopped reacting the way they did in March. That doesn''t mean the risk is gone. It means investors have adjusted their expectations based on the pattern so far. Your portfolio is positioned for the range of outcomes here, not for a bet on which way this particular headline resolves.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated exposure to TSX energy or gold mining names who have ridden several months of headline-driven swings and may be underestimating how much of that exposure is still headline dependent.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced portfolio holders who have seen little net movement and may be assuming, incorrectly, that the underlying conflict has become financially irrelevant.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with a systematic rebalancing discipline, since a gap between commodity repricing and broad equity calm can create entry points the emotional investor talks themselves out of.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review each client''s actual look-through exposure to Hormuz-linked names, not their perceived exposure based on headline recall.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check whether any client is treating the calm VIX reading as a signal to add risk near term.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any conversation where a client raises the disconnect between oil headlines and portfolio movement.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag concentrated energy or gold mining positions for a rebalancing review at the next scheduled check-in.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why markets look calmer than the headlines this week<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
You may have seen this week''s news on the Strait of Hormuz situation. I wanted to flag something specific: broad market volatility measures have actually stayed low through this latest escalation, even though oil prices have moved sharply. That gap reflects how markets have adjusted to several rounds of similar headlines since February, not a judgment that the underlying situation is resolved.<br><br>
Your portfolio remains positioned for a range of outcomes here rather than a bet on any single headline. I am watching your specific exposure to the sectors most directly affected and will reach out if anything changes that assessment.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors confused by the disconnect:</strong> They see oil headlines escalating, check their own portfolio or a broad index fund, and see little movement. Some conclude the news does not matter. Others become anxious that they are missing something, with no one to call and explain it.</p>
<p><strong>Single-metric risk watchers:</strong> Self-directed investors who track the VIX as their primary risk gauge are currently being told by that one number that everything is fine, a position that has real gaps this specific market is exposing.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<p>Have you noticed the VIX has barely moved this week despite the Hormuz situation getting worse? That gap between what oil is pricing and what equity volatility is pricing is actually the more interesting story right now.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor watching a single volatility number gets a partial picture at exactly the moment a partial picture is most costly. This week''s market is pricing genuine physical supply risk into oil while treating broader equity risk as resolved, a split that a self-directed investor checking one dashboard is unlikely to catch. An advisor''s value here is not predicting which way the Hormuz conflict resolves. It is making sure a client''s actual exposure, not their headline-driven sense of exposure, is understood and appropriately sized before the gap between commodity and equity pricing closes.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you currently tracking whether world events are actually affecting your portfolio versus just the headlines you''re seeing?</p>
<p>Do you know your specific exposure to energy or gold mining names right now, or a rough sense of your overall portfolio composition?</p>
<p>If oil prices kept climbing through the rest of the year, do you have a clear sense of how that would flow through to what you hold?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through how your portfolio is positioned for a scenario where this conflict doesn''t follow the same pattern as the last three escalations?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The gap between oil and the VIX this week<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
With the Strait of Hormuz situation escalating again this week, I noticed something worth a quick conversation: oil prices are moving sharply, but broader market volatility measures have stayed unusually calm. That gap says something specific about how markets are pricing this conflict, and it is worth understanding how it applies to your own holdings.<br><br>
Happy to walk through it if useful.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
