<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The federal small business deduction limit falls by $5 for every $1 of adjusted aggregate investment income above $50,000, reaching zero at $150,000. The reduction applies to the tax year following the one in which the AAII was earned.</p>
<h2>The 30-Day Trap Waiting Inside This Week''s Whipsaw</h2>
<p>The superficial loss rule denies a capital loss, adding it back to the cost base of the repurchased shares instead, if the taxpayer or an affiliated person acquires an identical property within 30 days before or after the sale and still holds it 30 days after the sale. Affiliated persons include a spouse or common-law partner and a corporation controlled by the taxpayer, which means a loss sold inside the CCPC can be denied if the shareholder personally, or their spouse, buys the same shares back within the window.</p>
<p>This week''s gold price action makes that trap unusually easy to walk into. A position sold at a loss on Monday''s 2.61% drop and repurchased on Tuesday''s near 2% rebound, inside the same window that produced both moves, would run headlong into the 30-day rule. The loss needs to actually stay realized, which means the position needs to stay out of the household and any controlled corporation for the full 30 days on both sides of the sale.</p>
<h2>What Doesn''t Apply Inside a TFSA</h2>
<p>None of this mechanism touches a TFSA. Losses realized inside a TFSA are not deductible against anything, in the corporation or personally, because TFSA gains and losses fall entirely outside the taxable income system. A client who holds the same gold miner inside both a TFSA and a CCPC non-registered account needs to understand that only the corporate position carries any tax-loss value. The TFSA loss, however real the dollar decline feels, simply disappears from a planning perspective.</p>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Business owner clients watching their corporate portfolio this week are likely feeling good about the energy side and mildly annoyed about the gold miner side, without connecting either to next year''s corporate tax return. There is no urgency in how it feels day to day, which is exactly why the planning window quietly closes without anyone noticing.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The energy names in your corporate account have had a strong run this week, and that''s a good problem to have. There''s a mechanical piece worth flagging though: realized gains inside the corporation this year become part of a number called AAII, and once that number crosses $50,000 for the year, it starts reducing your small business deduction, but not this year''s, next year''s. The good news is you have a lever. If there are loss positions elsewhere in the corporate account, realizing them in the same calendar year can offset that number before it locks in. I want to look at where your full-year AAII is tracking before December so we''re not caught by it in 2027.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Incorporated business owners with a CCPC holding a non-registered investment portfolio that includes this week''s energy or gold mining movers.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Business owners whose corporate portfolio is diversified enough that this week''s sector-specific moves are a small share of total AAII.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with an active holding company and a disciplined advisor who can identify and execute same-year loss harvesting before the calendar closes.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull year-to-date realized gains inside each incorporated client''s corporate investment account, with a focus on energy positions sold or trimmed this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify any loss positions, including gold mining names, that could be realized in the same 2026 tax year to offset AAII.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm no repurchase of an identical or near-identical security occurs within 30 days of any loss sale, across the client, their spouse, and any associated corporation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients approaching the $50,000 or $150,000 AAII thresholds for a dedicated year-end planning conversation before December 31.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A tax mechanic worth flagging on this week''s corporate account gains<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Your corporate account has benefited from this week''s move in energy stocks, which is good news. I wanted to flag a mechanical detail: realized gains this year affect your small business deduction next year, not this year, through a rule called the AAII passive income grind. It only bites once the year''s total crosses $50,000, and there may be an opportunity to manage that number using loss positions elsewhere in the account before year end.<br><br>
Let''s set up time before December to review where things stand.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated professionals and business owners:</strong> Doctors, dentists, lawyers and small business owners who hold a corporate investment portfolio inside their operating or holding company and manage it without a coordinated tax strategy.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed CCPC owners:</strong> Business owners who trade their corporate account themselves, often unaware that a rule most personal investors never encounter is quietly shaping next year''s corporate tax bill.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<p>If your corporation holds an investment portfolio, this week''s oil rally may have just added to a number that determines how much of your small business deduction survives next year, not this year.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A business owner managing their own corporate portfolio is optimizing for returns, which is the wrong single objective. The passive income grind means a strong year for corporate investment gains can quietly cost tens of thousands of dollars in lost small business deduction the following year, and the only lever to manage it, same-year loss realization, has a hard December 31 deadline and a 30-day repurchase trap that catches people who don''t know to look for it. An advisor''s value is coordinating the investment decision with the corporate tax mechanics before the calendar year closes, not after.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Does your corporation currently hold an investment portfolio outside of the operating business?</p>
<p>Has anyone reviewed how this year''s realized gains inside the corporation might affect your small business deduction next year?</p>
<p>Are you aware that capital losses only offset that calculation if realized in the same tax year as the gains?</p>
<p>Who is currently coordinating the investment decisions in your corporate account with your year-end corporate tax planning?</p>
</div>
