for (var g = 0; g < gridVals.length; g++){
  var gy = yp(gridVals[g]);
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1:margin.left, x2:margin.left+PW, y1:gy, y2:gy, stroke:"#ececec", "stroke-width":"0.5"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:margin.left-6, y:gy+3, "text-anchor":"end", "font-size":"8.5", "font-family":fontStack, fill:"#aaaaaa"}, gridVals[g]+"%"));
}
var barW = (PW / n) * 0.6;
for (var i = 0; i < n; i++){
  var cx = xp(i);
  var v = data[i].v;
  var barY = v >= 0 ? yp(v) : zeroY;
  var barH = Math.abs(yp(v) - zeroY);
  var fill = v >= 0 ? "#3a7a55" : "#8a3030";
  svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x:cx-barW/2, y:barY, width:barW, height:barH, fill:fill}));
  var labelY = v >= 0 ? (barY - 4) : (barY + barH + 10);
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:cx, y:labelY, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"7", "font-weight":"700", "font-family":fontStack, fill: (Math.abs(v) > 8 ? "#ffffff" : "#444444")}, (v>0?"+":"")+v.toFixed(1)+"%"));
  if (v < -6){
    svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:cx, y:barY+14, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"7", "font-weight":"700", "font-family":fontStack, fill:"#ffffff"}, (v>0?"+":"")+v.toFixed(1)+"%"));
  }
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:cx, y:margin.top+PH+13, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"7", "font-family":fontStack, fill:"#999999"}, data[i].l));
}
svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1:margin.left, x2:margin.left+PW, y1:zeroY, y2:zeroY, stroke:"#999999", "stroke-width":"1"}));
var fnIdx = 11;
var fnX = xp(fnIdx);
var fnBarBottom = yp(data[fnIdx].v);
var pillW = 46, pillH = 15;
var pillX = fnX - pillW/2;
var pillY = fnBarBottom - pillH - 16;
svg.appendChild(el("circle", {cx:fnX, cy:fnBarBottom, r:3.5, fill:"#8a3030"}));
svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x:pillX, y:pillY, width:pillW, height:pillH, rx:3, fill:"#e8a825"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:fnX, y:pillY+pillH/2+3.5, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"8", "font-weight":"700", "font-family":fontStack, fill:"#111111"}, "WIDEST MOVE"));
if (_cs && _cs.parentNode){ _cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg); }
})();</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Investing.com, Trading Economics, BBN Times, single-session data for Monday, July 13 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Oil and volatility repriced sharply the same day gold miners fell and the TSX Composite barely moved, showing the market treating this as a commodity-specific shock rather than a broad one.</p>
<h2>The Base Case Versus the Tail Risk for Canadian Portfolios</h2>
<p>The base case remains what it has been through three prior escalation cycles since February: a transit-risk premium that lifts Canadian energy producers, pressures gold miners on Fed-hawkishness expectations, and leaves the broader TSX largely untouched. Brent''s move above $85 this week, more than 15% above where it sat before this month''s re-escalation, fits that base case. It remains well below the nearly $120 the conflict reached at its most severe point in the spring.</p>
<p>The tail risk is narrower but more consequential: a genuine shift from transit disruption to production disruption, where Gulf Arab exporters beyond Iran see their own infrastructure targeted or their own militaries drawn into direct exchanges. That scenario would not simply extend the current oil rally, it would risk the kind of physical supply loss that pushes crude toward its wartime peak and drags global growth down with it, a combination that would hurt the broader TSX and Canadian exports far more than a higher oil price alone would help the energy sector. Kuwait''s drilling platform and this week''s unclaimed strikes are the first concrete data points suggesting that shift, not confirmation that it has happened.</p>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients following the headlines are alarmed by the casualty figures and the scale of this week''s strikes, and may not realize how narrowly the actual market reaction has been contained so far. Clients with concentrated energy exposure feel validated by the rally. Clients with broader portfolios may be confused why their statements look largely unchanged despite what sounds like a major war escalation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">What changed this week is not just the intensity of the strikes, it''s the type of threat Iran is making. Up to now this has been about Iran''s ability to disrupt shipping through one strait, and markets have priced that as a contained, specific risk. This week Iran threatened to halt energy exports from the whole region, which is a different and larger claim. Markets haven''t repriced for that broader scenario yet, and may never need to, but it''s the thing worth watching rather than the day-to-day headlines. Your portfolio''s energy exposure is benefiting from the current, more contained version of this risk. I''m watching for signs the situation moves toward the broader scenario.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated exposure to Canadian energy producers, currently benefiting from the base-case scenario, and clients with gold mining exposure absorbing losses from Fed-hawkishness expectations.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Broadly diversified clients whose portfolios have shown limited net movement despite the intensity of this week''s headlines.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients open to discussing tail-risk hedges given the genuine, if still low-probability, shift toward a broader regional conflict this week''s events represent.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client energy sector concentration against the base-case versus tail-risk framing in this article.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Watch for confirmed involvement of additional Gulf states beyond Iran and the US as a signal the base case is shifting.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document client conversations distinguishing the current contained oil rally from a genuine supply-disruption tail scenario.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any client asking about portfolio protection against a broader Middle East conflict for a dedicated planning conversation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What actually changed in the Middle East this week<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
This week''s headlines from the Strait of Hormuz situation are more intense than what we''ve seen in prior rounds, and I wanted to give you a clear read on what has and has not changed for your portfolio. The market''s reaction has so far stayed contained to energy and volatility markets rather than broadening out, which matches the pattern of prior escalations. I''m watching closely for any sign that changes.<br><br>
Happy to discuss further if you have questions.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors alarmed by headlines:</strong> Self-directed investors seeing casualty figures and blockade headlines and assuming their portfolio must be at serious risk, without a framework for distinguishing contained scenarios from genuine tail risk.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrated energy or gold investors:</strong> Self-directed holders of Canadian energy or gold mining names riding this week''s volatility without professional guidance on position sizing given the widening range of outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<p>Did you notice Iran''s threat this week actually changed in kind, not just in intensity, and that most portfolios still haven''t moved much despite it?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor reading this week''s headlines is likely to either overreact to casualty figures and blockade news that have not yet moved broad markets, or underreact to a genuine, if still unlikely, shift toward a wider regional conflict. Both mistakes come from not having a framework for separating the base case from the tail risk. An advisor''s value is not predicting how the Hormuz conflict resolves, it is making sure a client''s actual exposure is sized appropriately for the range of realistic outcomes, not just the headline of the day.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you currently distinguishing headline intensity from actual portfolio risk when you read Middle East coverage?</p>
<p>Do you know your specific exposure to Canadian energy and gold mining names right now?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through what would actually need to happen for this conflict to meaningfully change your portfolio''s risk profile?</p>
<p>If oil prices moved sharply higher on an actual supply disruption rather than a transit disruption, do you know how that would affect your holdings differently?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Middle East threat that actually changed this week<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
This week''s escalation in the Strait of Hormuz conflict included a genuinely new type of threat from Iran, one that markets have not yet fully priced. If you''re holding Canadian energy or gold mining positions, or just want a clear read on what this means for your broader portfolio, it''s worth a short conversation.<br><br>
Happy to walk through it.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
