<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX moved in a narrower range than the Nasdaq on both days this week, even as WTI and the VIX swung far more sharply than any equity index shown.</p>
<h2>The Bank Earnings Read-Through</h2>
<p>Tuesday''s US bank earnings were uniformly strong. JPMorgan posted its highest quarterly profit on record. Bank of America and Wells Fargo both beat estimates. Even so, JPMorgan fell 2.5% and Bank of America dropped 0.8% on the day, as broader risk-off sentiment from the Middle East and AI capex concerns outweighed the earnings beats. Wells Fargo sank 2% despite its own beat.</p>
<p>That disconnect, strong fundamentals overridden by macro sentiment, is worth watching as Canadian banks approach their own fiscal third-quarter reporting later this summer. If US banks can post record profits and still trade lower on the day, Canadian bank earnings will need more than a clean beat to move the TSX financials weighting, which remains the single largest sector driver of the index.</p>
<h2>What to Watch Into the Close</h2>
<p>Today''s session carries its own dual catalyst: the Bank of Canada''s rate decision and Monetary Policy Report at 9:45 a.m. Eastern, followed by Fed Chair Kevin Warsh''s Senate testimony at 10 a.m. Neither is expected to move the overnight rate itself, but both carry the kind of forward guidance that has moved bond yields, and by extension bank and rate-sensitive stocks, more than the headline decisions themselves in recent sessions.</p>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with US-heavy portfolios may feel whiplash from this week''s swings in tech and semiconductor names, particularly if they saw Monday''s losses before Tuesday''s partial recovery. Clients concentrated in Canadian holdings are likely feeling comparatively calm, and may not realize how much more turbulent the US side of a balanced portfolio has been this week.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">This week has actually been two very different markets. Wall Street has been whipping around on AI spending worries and a couple of specific earnings shocks, IBM in particular. The TSX barely moved either day, because Toronto doesn''t carry the same tech exposure and its energy and financial names have mostly just tracked the oil story, which has been the real source of volatility this week. If your portfolio has both Canadian and US holdings, that''s likely showing up as very different performance on each side this week, and that''s expected given how differently the two markets are built right now.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated exposure to US semiconductor or enterprise software names, who experienced the sharpest swings of the week.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced Canada-US portfolios seeing a wide performance gap between the two sides this week.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients weighted toward Canadian energy and financials, who have ridden a comparatively calm week while still capturing the oil price tailwind.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review any client holding US semiconductor or software names for this week''s volatility and confirm it matches their risk tolerance.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the TSX''s comparative stability this week as a talking point for clients questioning Canadian equity allocation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag Canadian bank earnings season as a near-term catalyst given how US bank earnings were received this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Watch today''s Bank of Canada and Warsh testimony for any shift that could end the TSX''s comparatively calm week.</div>
</div>
